
Slide 1: Title – Test Failure to Jira Automation System
“Good [morning/afternoon], everyone.

Today, I’ll walk you through our solution — Test Failure to Jira Automation System.
This solution focuses on automating how test failures are handled and converted into actionable Jira updates, reducing manual effort and improving response time.”
Slide 2: What Problem It Solves
“Let’s first understand the problem we’re solving.
Currently, QA engineers manually create Jira issues for every failed test run. This process is repetitive and time-consuming.
At the same time, developers need immediate notification of failures. However, today this often involves manually commenting on related user stories or bugs, which delays response and increases dependency on QA.”
Slide 3: Workflow Overview
“To solve this, we designed a workflow powered by three specialized agents along with supporting tools.
These agents work together in sequence:
First, we analyze test failures from TestRail
Then, we map them with existing Jira issues
Finally, we take automated actions in Jira
This creates a seamless, end-to-end automation pipeline from test failure to issue resolution.”
Slide 4: Agent 1 – Test Failure Analyzer (TestRail)
“The first agent is the Test Failure Analyzer.
It connects to TestRail and fetches failed test cases using a dedicated tool.

It extracts key details like test case title, status, and section.
Most importantly, it filters only failed scenarios and prepares a structured output, which is passed to the next agent.”
Slide 5: Agent 2 – Jira Similar Issue Mapping
“The second agent focuses on identifying whether a similar issue already exists in Jira.
It searches Jira using the test case name and maps failures to existing tickets.

It also retrieves the current status of those issues — whether they are Done, In Progress, or To Do.
This information is then passed to the final decision-making agent.”
Slide 6: Agent 3 – Decision Automation Engine
“The third agent is the decision engine, which automates actions in Jira.
Based on the issue status:
If an issue exists and is closed, it reopens the issue and adds a comment
If an issue exists and is already open, it simply adds a comment
If no issue exists, it creates a new bug automatically
This ensures the right action is taken without any manual intervention.”
Slide 7: Conclusion
“To conclude:
Today, QA engineers manually analyze failures, search Jira, and decide next steps, which is inefficient.

Manual issue creation and lack of automated notifications slow down the overall response time.
Our solution automates the complete flow — from detecting a test failure to taking the right action in Jira — enabling faster resolution and improved collaboration between QA and development teams.”
Slide 8: Thank You
“Thank you for your time.

I’m happy to take any questions.”
 
